{"reference":"Amos 8","book":"Amos","chapter":8,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"language_literary","title":"The vision’s wordplay","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The basket of summer fruit is not presented as a botanical omen. Its force lies in a Hebrew sound association: qayits (“summer fruit”) resembles qets (“end”). The vision therefore turns the ordinary image of ripe fruit into an announcement that Israel’s end has arrived. It stands as the fourth of the book’s five visions.","verse_refs":["Amos 8:1-3","Amos 8:1-8","Amos 8:9-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_008_dcbe9429b62ccd994ba308a5"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Commerce and exploitation","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 4–6 describe more than isolated dishonesty. The merchants resent time set apart from trade, then reduce measures, raise prices, use false scales, exploit debt to acquire poor people, and sell inferior grain. The accusations portray a connected pattern in which commercial gain is pursued through practices that damage those with the least power. The passage should not be reduced either to a condemnation of wealth as such or to a call for private generosity detached from debt and labor abuse.","verse_refs":["Amos 8:1-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_008_c1df296e848863d9e1dc4fd9"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Judgment imagery and silence","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The chapter joins social injustice to images of a destabilized world: the land trembles and rises like a river, daylight darkens, feasts become mourning, and the people face thirst. This connection does not authorize assigning particular disasters to the particular sins of those who suffer them. The later “famine” is specifically a famine of hearing Jehovah’s words: people search but do not find them. Within Amos 8, it is an image of judgment and futile searching, not grounds for leaders to control access to revelation or to label every disagreement divine silence.","verse_refs":["Amos 8:1-3","Amos 8:1-8","Amos 8:8-10","Amos 8:9-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_008_fff407ba51e1b5759ff24ebe"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}